WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.020
Cut a mouse nerve.

2
00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:10.080
The part of each axon beyond the cut looks normal for about a day.

3
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.220
Then it falls to pieces.

4
00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:18.960
In one strain of mouse, the same cut axons stay intact for two weeks or more.

5
00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:21.820
What is different about that mouse?

6
00:00:22.583 --> 00:00:27.543
The axon is the long process that carries a neuron's output.

7
00:00:28.223 --> 00:00:30.163
The cell body holds the nucleus.

8
00:00:31.283 --> 00:00:36.303
People long assumed a cut stump simply starves, like a severed branch.

9
00:00:37.163 --> 00:00:44.583
But mice that lack one gene, called Sarm1, keep cut axons intact for a long time.

10
00:00:45.403 --> 00:00:50.023
If one mutation can stop the breakdown, something is running it.

11
00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:52.823
Here is how the program starts.

12
00:00:53.683 --> 00:00:59.803
An enzyme called NMNAT2 works in the axon, and it is short-lived.

13
00:01:00.743 --> 00:01:05.883
The cell body must keep sending it down the axon, like trucks refilling a tank.

14
00:01:06.703 --> 00:01:08.383
A cut closes the road.

15
00:01:08.783 --> 00:01:13.883
The supply stops, and NMNAT2 in the stump runs low.

16
00:01:14.917 --> 00:01:20.737
With NMNAT2 gone, its raw material, NMN, builds up.

17
00:01:21.417 --> 00:01:23.537
And NAD+ begins to fall.

18
00:01:24.637 --> 00:01:28.717
NMNAT enzymes make NAD+ from NMN.

19
00:01:29.797 --> 00:01:33.857
A protein called SARM1 waits in the axon, held shut.

20
00:01:34.777 --> 00:01:40.237
In the leading model, a rise in NMN, relative to NAD+, switches it on.

21
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.280
SARM1 is an enzyme.

22
00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:47.440
Once switched on, it cleaves NAD+.

23
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.720
Then ATP falls. As energy fails, calcium rises inside the axon.

24
00:01:55.640 --> 00:01:59.460
Calcium switches on calpains, enzymes that cut proteins.

25
00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.940
The calpains cut the cytoskeleton, and the axon fragments.

26
00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.740
All of this happens in a stump with no nucleus.

27
00:02:08.733 --> 00:02:11.373
Now the slow mouse makes sense.

28
00:02:11.993 --> 00:02:17.853
Its Wlds protein is a longer-lived source of NMNAT activity.

29
00:02:18.393 --> 00:02:21.073
It keeps the tank full after the trucks stop.

30
00:02:22.413 --> 00:02:26.313
Delete SARM1 instead, and cut axons survive too.

31
00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:28.553
So the stump is not starving.

32
00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:32.893
It is running a program, and a program has parts that can be stopped.
